    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 270441\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n845 PM PST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Warming temperatures can be expected through the weekend. Breezy \n  north winds along the Colorado River Valley will ease Friday, with \n  light winds elsewhere. \n\n* A Pacific storm could bring gusty winds, widespread rainfall and \n  mountain snow next week, with the best precipitation chances \n  currently Tuesday into Wednesday. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...This afternoon through Wednesday.\n\nA building ridge of high pressure will bring generally quiet weather \nacross the Desert Southwest through the holiday weekend. Most areas \nwill continue to see sunny skies and light winds today and Friday, \nwith the exception of the lower Colorado River Valley, where breezy \nnorth winds will continue through the afternoon. Winds may gust as \nhigh as 30 MPH near Laughlin and Bullhead City and could produce \nwaves up to 3 feet on Lake Mohave. Winds along the river will ease \non Friday, although a few gusts up to 20 MPH will remain possible. \nTemperatures will continue to warm through Sunday, with highs \nreaching 8 to 10 degrees above normal by the end of the weekend.  \n\nMore unsettled conditions are expected early next week as a trough \nof low pressure pushes into the region from the Pacific. There \ncontinues to be quite a bit of uncertainty with the timing and track \nof the trough, and the latest model runs have continued to slow its \narrival and shift the track farther south. Strong, gusty winds, \nwidespread rainfall and high-elevation snow are possible, although \nthe details of where and when these impacts occur will depend \nheavily on the system's eventual track through the region. Right \nnow, the best chances for precipitation appear to be Tuesday into \nWednesday, but this timing is subject to change. Rainfall totals \ncould range from 0.25 to 0.50\" in desert valleys to around 1\" in the \nmountains, with up to 1 foot of snow possible in the higher \nelevations of the Sierra. These amounts will depend on the storm's \neventual track and timing. Continue to monitor the forecast over the \nnext several days as the storm's details become clearer and forecast \nconfidence improves. \n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill remain under 10 knots through the TAF period, favoring \ntypical daily directional trends. VFR conditions will continue \nthrough the TAF period with periods of mid-to-high cloud cover.\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...With the exception of \nthe lower Colorado River Valley, winds will remain under 10 knots \nand will favor typical daily directional trends through the TAF \nperiod. Elevated northerly winds will persist in the Colorado River \nValley, mainly focused near KIFP where afternoon gusts of 20 - 25 \nknots are expected. Otherwise, VFR conditions will persist through \nthe TAF period with periods of mid and high cloud cover. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Planz\nAVIATION...Stessman/CO\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
